    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "4c36f8e0-86c0-5637-a947-6dfad6507d8f",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x,y)$ varies with both $x$ and $y,$ which of the following **cannot** be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope field varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, we note the following slope field does not vary with $y$ given a fixed value of $x.$\n\n![](images/9e/9e237b1ed82702a83a9dd57bae1b8b145869b6553a3d1d3cf46bdb462f1185c1.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line, the slopes do not vary.\n\n![](images/9e/9ea58ec780f3197256943f3a1ed62537364653e563ed214b33b6b716ec3bb19d.png)",
